<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<quiz>
  <question type="stack">
    <name>
      <text>Find and classify the stationary points of the graph of 𝑓(𝑥)</text>
    </name>
    <questiontext format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<p>Consider the function \(f(x) = {@fx@}\)</p>
<p>i. Find and classify the stationary points of the graph of \(f(x)\). Enter your answers as points, in fractions where appropriate, in the relevant boxes and if there are none simply type in the word "none". Give your answer correct to 2 decimal places where appropriate.</p>
<p>Maximum: \( \big( \) [[input:ans1]],[[input:ans2]] \( \big) \) [[validation:ans1]][[validation:ans2]][[feedback:prt1]][[feedback:prt2]]</p>
<p>Minimum: \( \big( \) [[input:ans3]],[[input:ans4]] \( \big) \)[[validation:ans3]][[validation:ans4]][[feedback:prt3]][[feedback:prt4]]</p>
<p>Stationary point of inflection: \( \big( \) [[input:ans5]],[[input:ans6]] \( \big) \)[[validation:ans5]][[validation:ans6]][[feedback:prt5]][[feedback:prt6]]</p>]]></text>
    </questiontext>
    <generalfeedback format="html">
      <text><![CDATA[<h3>Worked Solution</h3>
<p>To find stationary points we need to find the values of \(x\) for which \(f'(x) = 0\).</p><p>\(f'(x) = {@dfx@} = 0\)</p><p>\({@factor(dfx)@} = 0\)</p><p>\(x={@x1@}\) or \(x={@x2@}\) or \(x={@x3@}\).</p><p>We now need to classify these points. To do so we use the following table:</p>
<table>
<caption></caption>
<thead>
<tr><th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">\(x=\)</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">before {@dispdp(x1,2)@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(x1,2)@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">after {@dispdp(x1,2)@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">before {@x2@}<br></th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@x2@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">after {@x2@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">before {@dispdp(x3,2)@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(x3,2)@}</th>
<th scope="col" style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">after {@dispdp(x3,2)@}</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">\(f'(x)=\)</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(bx1,2)@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">0</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(ax1,2)@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(bx2,2)@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">0</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(ax2,2)@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(bx3,2)@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">0</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@dispdp(ax3,2)@}</td>
</tr>
<tr>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; "><br></td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behbx1@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">stationary</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behax1@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behbx2@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">stationary</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behax2@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behbx3@}</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">stationary</td>
<td style="text-align: center;border: 1px solid black; ">{@behax3@}</td>
</tr>
</tbody></table><br><p>Since before \(x={@dispdp(x1,2)@}\) the graph is {@behbx1@} and after it is {@behax1@}, we have a minimum. Once we find the value of \(f({@dispdp(x1,2)@})\) we conclude that the local minimum point is \( ({@dispdp(taminx,2)@},{@dispdp(taminy,2)@})\).</p><p>Similarly, before \(x={@x2@}\)&nbsp;the graph is {@behbx2@} and after it is {@behax2@}, we have a stationary point of inflection. Once we find the value of \(f({@x2@})\) we conclude that the stationary point of inflection is \(({@tainfx@},{@tainfy@})\).<br></p><p>Finally, as before \(x={@dispdp(x3,2)@}\)&nbsp;the graph is {@behbx3@} and after it is {@behax3@}, we have a maximum. Once we find the value of \(f({@dispdp(x3,2)@})\) we conclude that the local maximum point is \(({@dispdp(tamaxx,2)@},{@dispdp(tamaxy,2)@})\).</p>]]></text>
    </generalfeedback>
